1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,506
In March 2025,

2
00:00:02,506 --> 00:00:10,338
a robot six hundred metres beneath the Southern Ocean filmed something nobody had ever seen alive in the wild.

3
00:00:10,338 --> 00:00:12,618
A colossal squid.

4
00:00:12,618 --> 00:00:15,982
It was only a baby, about thirty centimetres long.

5
00:00:15,982 --> 00:00:18,751
But adults are thought to reach around seven metres,

6
00:00:18,751 --> 00:00:21,577
and weigh up to half a tonne.

7
00:00:21,577 --> 00:00:25,799
Their eyes are believed to be the largest of any animal alive.

8
00:00:25,799 --> 00:00:28,958
And that's the strange rule of the deep sea.

9
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:33,535
The further down you go, the more often you meet giants.

10
00:00:33,535 --> 00:00:34,978
So let's dive.

11
00:00:34,978 --> 00:00:36,431
All the way down.

12
00:00:36,431 --> 00:00:38,003
To find out why.

13
00:00:38,003 --> 00:00:40,326
And to find the invisible line,

14
00:00:40,326 --> 00:00:43,785
about eight kilometres down, where even fish give up.

15
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,620
Zero metres.

16
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:56,121
Sunlight, warmth, and life at the size we expect.

17
00:00:56,121 --> 00:00:58,214
But within a few hundred metres,

18
00:00:58,214 --> 00:01:01,160
the ocean starts playing with scale.

19
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:02,656
Off the coast of Japan,

20
00:01:02,656 --> 00:01:08,829
a crab walks the seafloor on legs that can stretch almost four metres from claw to claw.

21
00:01:08,829 --> 00:01:12,090
That's longer than two people lying end to end.

22
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:16,041
The Japanese spider crab is the largest crab alive.

23
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:17,314
And it's only the beginning.

24
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:21,271
Two hundred metres.

25
00:01:21,271 --> 00:01:23,683
Most of the sunlight is gone.

26
00:01:23,683 --> 00:01:26,386
Welcome to the twilight zone.

27
00:01:26,386 --> 00:01:29,784
Here lives a fish that looks like a silver ribbon.

28
00:01:29,784 --> 00:01:33,380
The giant oarfish can grow to about eight metres,

29
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,821
the longest bony fish on Earth.

30
00:01:35,821 --> 00:01:38,561
It has been seen hanging upright in the water,

31
00:01:38,561 --> 00:01:42,701
rippling a red fin along its back, like a living banner.

32
00:01:42,701 --> 00:01:46,481
But even the oarfish isn't the longest thing out here.

33
00:01:46,481 --> 00:01:52,970
In 2020, explorers in deep canyons off Western Australia came across a siphonophore,

34
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:55,257
coiled into a giant spiral.

35
00:01:55,257 --> 00:01:59,014
Its outer ring was estimated at around forty-five metres.

36
00:01:59,014 --> 00:02:01,876
That's longer than a blue whale.

37
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:02,759
And here's the twist.

38
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:04,512
It isn't one animal.

39
00:02:04,512 --> 00:02:07,240
It's a colony of countless tiny clones,

40
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,392
each with its own job, working together as one body.

41
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:14,749
One thousand metres.

42
00:02:14,749 --> 00:02:17,406
The last of the sunlight is gone.

43
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:18,451
From here down,

44
00:02:18,451 --> 00:02:21,788
the only light comes from the animals themselves.

45
00:02:21,788 --> 00:02:25,419
The water is close to freezing, and food is rare.

46
00:02:25,419 --> 00:02:27,380
Most of it is marine snow,

47
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:31,113
flakes of dead material drifting down from above.

48
00:02:31,113 --> 00:02:34,508
This is where the giants get strange.

49
00:02:34,508 --> 00:02:36,060
In the cold,

50
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:39,354
dark waters of the far north lives the Greenland shark.

51
00:02:39,354 --> 00:02:42,580
It can grow over six metres long,

52
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:44,921
but it grows less than a centimetre a year.

53
00:02:44,921 --> 00:02:48,960
When scientists carbon-dated the lenses of their eyes,

54
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:54,878
the biggest one came out somewhere between two hundred and seventy-two and five hundred and twelve years old.

55
00:02:54,878 --> 00:02:56,880
Even at the low end,

56
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,098
it was born before the United States existed.

57
00:03:00,098 --> 00:03:03,591
On the seafloor, you'll find something oddly familiar.

58
00:03:03,591 --> 00:03:06,171
A relative of the little woodlouse in your garden,

59
00:03:06,171 --> 00:03:08,603
grown to nearly half a metre.

60
00:03:08,603 --> 00:03:10,690
The giant isopod.

61
00:03:10,690 --> 00:03:16,233
One, at an aquarium in Japan, ate a single fish in January 2009.

62
00:03:16,233 --> 00:03:20,283
Then it refused food for five years and forty-three days.

63
00:03:22,567 --> 00:03:25,688
So why are there so many giants down here?

64
00:03:25,688 --> 00:03:28,730
Scientists call it deep-sea gigantism.

65
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:32,494
And the honest answer is, there's no single explanation.

66
00:03:32,494 --> 00:03:34,668
There are four leading ideas.

67
00:03:34,668 --> 00:03:35,344
One.

68
00:03:35,344 --> 00:03:36,335
The cold.

69
00:03:36,335 --> 00:03:39,249
Near-freezing water slows everything down.

70
00:03:39,249 --> 00:03:43,442
Animals grow slowly, live longer, and keep on growing.

71
00:03:43,442 --> 00:03:44,164
Two.

72
00:03:44,164 --> 00:03:45,189
Oxygen.

73
00:03:45,189 --> 00:03:47,085
Cold water can hold more of it,

74
00:03:47,085 --> 00:03:54,363
and a major study of almost two thousand species of amphipods found that their maximum size rises with oxygen.

75
00:03:54,363 --> 00:03:55,204
Three.

76
00:03:55,204 --> 00:03:56,126
Hunger.

77
00:03:56,126 --> 00:03:58,905
A bigger body burns less energy per kilogram,

78
00:03:58,905 --> 00:04:00,486
and stores more fuel.

79
00:04:00,486 --> 00:04:02,597
When your next meal could be months away,

80
00:04:02,597 --> 00:04:04,648
size is a survival strategy.

81
00:04:04,648 --> 00:04:07,466
Just ask that isopod.

82
00:04:07,466 --> 00:04:07,766
Four.

83
00:04:07,766 --> 00:04:08,485
Time.

84
00:04:08,485 --> 00:04:10,979
With fewer predators and very long lives,

85
00:04:10,979 --> 00:04:13,649
there is simply more time to get big.

86
00:04:13,649 --> 00:04:16,062
But here's what most videos won't tell you.

87
00:04:16,062 --> 00:04:18,334
Giants are not the rule down here.

88
00:04:18,334 --> 00:04:20,663
Most life on the deep seafloor is tiny,

89
00:04:20,663 --> 00:04:22,414
smaller than a millimetre.

90
00:04:22,414 --> 00:04:24,725
Gigantism shows up in certain groups,

91
00:04:24,725 --> 00:04:27,298
like crustaceans, squid and some fish.

92
00:04:27,298 --> 00:04:29,352
The deep doesn't make everything big.

93
00:04:29,352 --> 00:04:31,379
It makes a few things enormous.

94
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:35,492
Six thousand metres.

95
00:04:35,492 --> 00:04:37,224
We enter the trenches.

96
00:04:37,224 --> 00:04:39,169
The hadal zone, named after Hades.

97
00:04:39,169 --> 00:04:41,029
At eight thousand metres,

98
00:04:41,029 --> 00:04:47,329
the water pressure is around eight hundred times what you feel at the surface.

99
00:04:47,329 --> 00:04:55,919
And yet, in 2023, scientists revealed footage of a small, pale snailfish swimming at eight thousand,

100
00:04:55,919 --> 00:04:58,510
three hundred and thirty-six metres, in a trench off Japan.

101
00:04:58,510 --> 00:05:01,388
The deepest fish ever filmed.

102
00:05:01,388 --> 00:05:04,258
It may be about as deep as a fish can go.

103
00:05:04,258 --> 00:05:09,475
Fish rely on a molecule called TMAO to keep their proteins working under pressure.

104
00:05:09,475 --> 00:05:15,188
Scientists estimate that somewhere between eight thousand two hundred and eight thousand four hundred metres,

105
00:05:15,188 --> 00:05:18,060
a fish can no longer hold enough of it.

106
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:20,178
That's the invisible line.

107
00:05:20,178 --> 00:05:23,183
Below it, no fish has ever been found.

108
00:05:23,183 --> 00:05:27,499
Only shrimp-like amphipods, other invertebrates and microbes carry on,

109
00:05:27,499 --> 00:05:32,063
all the way to the bottom of the Challenger Deep, nearly eleven kilometres down.

110
00:05:34,367 --> 00:05:37,028
And here's the most humbling part.

111
00:05:37,028 --> 00:05:45,023
Scientists estimate that we have seen less than a thousandth of one percent of the deep seafloor with cameras.

112
00:05:45,023 --> 00:05:51,169
Every giant in this video was found by someone who looked a little deeper than before.

113
00:05:51,169 --> 00:05:54,966
So the real question isn't how big they get.

114
00:05:54,966 --> 00:05:59,068
It's what's still down there, that we haven't met yet.

115
00:05:59,068 --> 00:06:01,753
If this dive blew your mind, subscribe.

116
00:06:01,753 --> 00:06:04,348
We'll take you somewhere even stranger next time.
